 ### Fixed
 
 - `onError` and `afterSession` hooks now fire when `beforeSession` throws, matching the guarantee the hook names imply. Previously, a throw from `beforeSession` escaped the `try`/`finally` block entirely, so neither hook ran. **Migration note**: if your `afterSession` hook assumed it would only be called after a full session turn, it will now also be called when `beforeSession` itself fails — the hook can inspect the session to detect this case (no entries beyond the initial user message will have been processed).
+- `onError` now fires for every rejection inside a turn, not only for rejections that are `Error` instances. A provider, hook, or tool that throws a string, a plain object, or `undefined` used to bypass `onError` entirely while the rejection still reached the caller, so a deployer counting failures or paging through the hook saw none of these. The caught value now reaches the hook as an `Error`: the original instance when it already is one, otherwise a wrapper whose `message` is a readable rendering (the thrown string itself, or JSON for an object) and whose `cause` is the original value, so a hook typed against `Error` keeps compiling and a hook that wants the raw value reads `cause`. **Migration note**: an `onError` hook will now also be called where it previously was not — a hook that counts, pages, or writes incident records sees strictly more calls, and all of them are failures it was silently missing; the value the caller receives as the rejection is unchanged.
 
